`DataSortOrder` values:

| Value        | Behaviour                                        |
| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------ |
| `"unsorted"` | Bars appear in the order provided by the dataset |
| `"desc"`     | Highest value first (left) → lowest value last   |
| `"asc"`      | Lowest value first (left) → highest value last   |

Sorting is applied before overflow grouping, so "Others" still works correctly:

```tsx
<VerticalBarChart
  data={manyCategories}
  barGroupConfig={{
    dataSort: "desc",
    categoryLabels: true,
    categoryLabelConfig: {
      enableOverflowGrouping: true,
      othersPosition: "end",
    },
  }}
/>
```

The underlying utility `sortByValue()` from `src/utils/dataTransformers.ts` is generic and reusable across all chart types.

## Design Tokens Used

The chart-level shell reads a single CSS custom property directly (its section gap); the rest of the design-token surface lives in the Layer 3 children. See [VerticalBarGroup.md](../../groups/VerticalBarGroup/VerticalBarGroup.md), [ChartHeader.md](../../groups/ChartHeader/ChartHeader.md), [ChartFooter.md](../../groups/ChartFooter/ChartFooter.md), [ChartLegend.md](../../groups/ChartLegend/ChartLegend.md), and [DataDisplay.md](../../groups/DataDisplay/DataDisplay.md) for the full token surface of each child.

| Property           | Token                                                   | Fallback                  | Description                                                                                                                                       |
| ------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Section Gap        | `--VerticalBarChart-gap` (falls back to `--Spacing-5`)      | `20px` (via `--Spacing-5`)    | Vertical spacing between chart sections (header / legend / DataDisplay / bar group / footer), a plain CSS `gap` in `VerticalBarChart.module.css` |
| Categorical (bold) | `--DataVis-Categorical-Bold-{n}`                            | none (always resolves)       | 1-based categorical colour helper (`catColor(index)`) used by the auto-generated legend                                                          |

### Legend colour resolution

Legend auto-generation resolves each item's colour as `d.color ?? catColor(1)` — i.e. the data row's own `color` if set, otherwise `var(--DataVis-Categorical-Bold-1)`. There is no third fallback and no hex literal anywhere in this component; `catColor()` always returns a valid CSS custom property reference.

## Legend Auto-Generation

When `chartLegend` is `true` and no `chartLegendConfig.items` are provided, the component auto-generates legend items:

1. **If `chartLegendConfig.categoryLabelValue` is provided**: Creates a single ChartKey with that label and the first bar's color (or `var(--DataVis-Categorical-Bold-1)`)

2. **If data has custom colors**: Groups data by unique colors and creates a ChartKey for each unique color (max 6 items)

3. **Default behavior**: Creates a single ChartKey using the first data point's label and `var(--DataVis-Categorical-Bold-1)` color

This ensures the legend stays connected to the actual chart data and colors.

## Height Behavior

The `height` prop controls the **total chart height**, not just the bar group. The VerticalBarGroup automatically stretches to fill the remaining space after header, legend, DataDisplay, and footer.

### How It Works

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ ChartHeader (natural height)            │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ ChartLegend (natural height, optional)  │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ DataDisplay (natural height, optional)  │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│                                         │
│ VerticalBarGroup (flex: 1, fills rest)  │
│                                         │
├─────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ ChartFooter (natural height)            │
└─────────────────────────────────────────┘
       ↑                                ↑
       └────── Total height prop ───────┘
```

### Usage Examples

```tsx
// Fixed 500px chart - bar group fills remaining space
<VerticalBarChart
  data={data}
  height={500}
  chartHeader={true}
  chartFooter={true}
/>

// Compact 250px chart for dashboards
<VerticalBarChart
  data={data}
  height={250}
  chartHeader={true}
  chartFooter={false}
/>

// Bars only - fills full height
<VerticalBarChart
  data={data}
  height={300}
  chartHeader={false}
  chartFooter={false}
/>
```

### Key Benefits

1. **Predictable sizing**: Chart always respects the specified height
2. **Automatic stretching**: Bar group expands to fill available space
3. **Consistent layouts**: Same chart height regardless of which sections are visible
4. **Responsive-friendly**: Use percentage heights for fluid layouts

## Hover Interaction

The VerticalBarChart supports hover interaction with automatic dimming of non-hovered bars.

### Color & Opacity States

| State                              | Color Token                      | Opacity | Description                   |
| ---------------------------------- | -------------------------------- | ------- | ----------------------------- |
| **Default** (nothing hovered)      | `categorical/bold/{colorIndex}`  | `1`     | Original vibrant colors       |
| **Highlighted** (this bar hovered) | `categorical/bold/{colorIndex}`  | `1`     | Stays vibrant                 |
| **Dimmed** (another bar hovered)   | `categorical/hover/{colorIndex}` | `0.2`   | Muted color + reduced opacity |

### Transition Tokens

All hover transitions use design tokens from the `hoverAnimation` collection:

- **Easing**: `"easing"` - Cubic-bezier timing function
- **Duration**: `"duration"` - Transition time (e.g., 200ms for "Moderate" mode)
- **Dim Opacity**: `"dimOpacity"` - Opacity for dimmed bars (default: 0.2)

### Hit Area Extension

When `interactive={true}` (default), each bar's interactive area automatically extends to fill the gap between adjacent bars, eliminating dead zones. This is computed by `VerticalBarGroup` and passed to individual bars via `hitAreaWidth`. The "Others" stacked bar shows the first (bottom) segment's data when hovered through the extended area.

See [VerticalBarGroup.md — Hit Area Extension](../../groups/VerticalBarGroup/VerticalBarGroup.md#hit-area-extension) for the full computation; the same pattern applies to other chart types built on `VerticalBarGroup`.

### Enabling/Disabling Interactivity

The `interactive` prop at the parent level controls hover behavior.

```tsx
// Interactive (default) - bars have hover effects
<VerticalBarChart
  data={data}
  interactive={true}
/>

// Non-interactive - no hover effects
<VerticalBarChart
  data={data}
  interactive={false}
/>
```

### Using Callbacks

### Hover Callback Data

```typescript
interface VerticalBarHoverData {
  index?: number; // Data point index
  label?: string; // Category label
  value: number; // Data value
  isHovered: boolean; // Current hover state
  mouseX?: number; // Mouse X coordinate relative to chart container
  mouseY?: number; // Mouse Y coordinate relative to chart container
}
