## Entry Animation

When `animated={true}`, bars grow from zero height to their full D3-scaled height on first load, staggered one after another.

### Enabling Entry Animation

```tsx
<VerticalBarChart data={data} animated={true} />
```

### How It Works

1. On first render, all bar heights are set to 0
2. After the first paint, bar heights transition to their D3-scaled values
3. Each bar has a staggered delay (`index × offsetDelay`), so they appear one after another
4. Duration and easing come from the `enterAnimation` Figma token collection

Powered by the shared `useEntryAnimation` hook (`../../../hooks/useEntryAnimation.ts`) — the same hook used by `VerticalBarGroup` and other chart components in this package.

### Combined with Other Features

```tsx
<VerticalBarChart
  data={data}
  animated={true}
  interactive={true}
  barGroupConfig={{
    yAxis: true,
    topDataLabels: true,
    hoverBadge: true,
  }}
/>
```

### Overflow Re-entry Behaviour

When `enableOverflowGrouping` is `false` and `labelWidth` is `"fluid"`, bars that don't fit on smaller screens are trimmed from the right. If the screen later becomes wider and those bars reappear, they are rendered at their full height **instantly** — no entry animation is replayed. This prevents a visual "expand from the middle" artefact that CSS transitions can cause on newly mounted elements.

Internally, `VerticalBarGroup` tracks the previous `scaledData.length` via a ref. When new bars appear at indices beyond the previous count (and the initial entry animation has already settled), the component passes `disableTransitions={true}` to those `VerticalBar` instances, which sets all CSS `transition` properties to `"none"`.

## Data Transition Animation

When `animated={true}` and the `data` prop changes after the chart has loaded, bars smoothly transition from their current heights to new heights. The AvgLine (when visible) also slides up or down to its recalculated position in sync. This uses the shared `useDataTransition` hook alongside entry animation.

### How It Works

1. On first render, entry animation plays (bars grow from zero)
2. After entry animation settles, the `transitionAnimation` tokens take over
3. Any subsequent data changes trigger smooth CSS transitions on bar heights
4. Bars update in a staggered wave pattern (left to right)
5. AvgLine smoothly slides to its new vertical position using `transitionAnimation` easing and duration
6. When switching between mixed (negative/positive) and positive-only data, bar bottom corners animate between flat and pill radius with direction-aware timing — see [VerticalBarGroup.md](../../groups/VerticalBarGroup/VerticalBarGroup.md#data-transition-animation) for full details

### Usage

```tsx
const [data, setData] = useState(initialData);

// Data changes trigger smooth animated transitions
<VerticalBarChart
  data={data}
  animated={true}
/>

// Later — update data, bars animate smoothly
<button onClick={() => setData(newData)}>Update Data</button>
```

### Design Tokens

| Property     | Token Name    | Collection            | Fallback                       |
| ------------ | ------------- | --------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Easing       | `easing`      | `transitionAnimation` | `cubic-bezier(0.5, 0, 0.3, 1)` |
| Duration     | `duration`    | `transitionAnimation` | `400ms`                        |
| Offset Delay | `offsetDelay` | `transitionAnimation` | `60ms`                         |
| Motion Mode  | `mode`        | `transitionAnimation` | `Moderate`                     |

Powered by the shared `useDataTransition` hook (`../../../hooks/useDataTransition.ts`).

## Sparse Data Edge Case

When the chart receives only 1-3 data entries, `VerticalBarGroup` automatically renders grey placeholder bars alongside the real data to indicate that more data is expected. The total bar count is always 5 (real + dummy). No additional props are needed — the behavior is automatic.

- **4+ entries**: Normal rendering
- **1-3 entries**: Real bars (left) + grey dummy bars (right, count = 5 − real), first dummy matches last real bar's height and is labeled "No entries yet"
- **0 entries**: Empty chart

```tsx
// 1 data entry → automatically shows 5 grey placeholder bars
<VerticalBarChart
  data={[{ category: "Jan", value: 80 }]}
  chartHeader={true}
  chartHeaderConfig={{ chartTitleValue: "Early Data" }}
  barGroupConfig={{ yAxis: true }}
/>
```

See [VerticalBarGroup.md — Sparse Data Edge Case](../../groups/VerticalBarGroup/VerticalBarGroup.md#sparse-data-edge-case-1-3-entries) for full details, and `../../../utils/sparseDataUtils.ts` for the underlying utility.

